1
00:00:00,588 --> 00:00:04,568
There's a three-digit number that
decides what you pay for a house, a car,

2
00:00:04,588 --> 00:00:08,350
an apartment… sometimes even
whether you get the job.

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:11,609
You never signed up for it.
You can't opt out of it.

4
00:00:11,629 --> 00:00:15,561
And three private companies are
building it about you right now.

5
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:17,641
They're not doing it as a favor.

6
00:00:17,661 --> 00:00:20,388
In this business, you're
not even the customer.

7
00:00:20,408 --> 00:00:21,770
You're the product.

8
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:25,390
By the end of this, you'll know who
invented this number, who's getting

9
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:29,920
rich from it, and why paying cash
for everything can actually hurt you.

10
00:00:33,559 --> 00:00:39,539
Atlanta, 1899. Two brothers, Cator
and Guy Woolford, start keeping lists

11
00:00:39,559 --> 00:00:43,983
for local grocers: who pays their
bills on time, and who doesn't.

12
00:00:44,212 --> 00:00:48,672
The lists sell so well, they turn them
into a company called Retail Credit.

13
00:00:48,692 --> 00:00:52,322
Its job was simple: find
out if you could be trusted.

14
00:00:52,342 --> 00:00:56,082
And the way it found out was by
sending people to ask about you.

15
00:00:56,102 --> 00:00:59,344
Your neighbors. Your landlord. Your boss.

16
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:03,383
Imagine it's 1968. You
apply for car insurance.

17
00:01:03,403 --> 00:01:07,593
And a stranger has already called your
neighbor to ask how much you drink.

18
00:01:07,794 --> 00:01:13,134
By the late sixties, Retail Credit had
around 7,000 investigators, and files

19
00:01:13,154 --> 00:01:15,759
on about 42 million people.

20
00:01:15,779 --> 00:01:19,919
Critics said those files covered your
marriage, your drinking, your sex

21
00:01:19,939 --> 00:01:22,363
life… even your politics.

22
00:01:22,673 --> 00:01:26,120
Some investigators had quotas
for negative information.

23
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:29,203
And you had no right to see
what they'd written about you.

24
00:01:29,273 --> 00:01:32,933
Then the company started planning
to put it all on computers.

25
00:01:32,953 --> 00:01:34,913
That's when Congress stepped in.

26
00:01:35,042 --> 00:01:39,982
In 1970, a new law, the Fair Credit
Reporting Act, finally gave you the

27
00:01:40,002 --> 00:01:43,743
right to see your file, and
to fight what was wrong in it.

28
00:01:43,932 --> 00:01:47,272
A few years later, Retail
Credit changed its name.

29
00:01:47,292 --> 00:01:50,872
You know it today as
Equifax. Remember that name.

30
00:01:50,892 --> 00:01:52,133
It comes back.

31
00:01:55,712 --> 00:01:59,372
But gossip is slow. And it's personal.

32
00:01:59,392 --> 00:02:02,593
Lenders wanted something
faster, and cheaper.

33
00:02:02,793 --> 00:02:06,213
Back then, a loan officer
often just decided.

34
00:02:06,233 --> 00:02:09,893
If he didn't like your clothes, your
neighborhood, or your last name, the

35
00:02:09,913 --> 00:02:11,133
answer was no.

36
00:02:11,272 --> 00:02:16,612
In 1956, an engineer named Bill Fair
and a mathematician named Earl Isaac

37
00:02:16,632 --> 00:02:21,172
started a company with a radical idea:
let a formula decide who pays you

38
00:02:21,192 --> 00:02:22,022
back.

39
00:02:22,042 --> 00:02:25,782
They wrote to the fifty biggest
lenders in America to pitch it.

40
00:02:25,802 --> 00:02:28,156
Forty-nine never answered.

41
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:33,065
One did. And that one reply
turned into an empire.

42
00:02:33,085 --> 00:02:37,465
In 1989, they launched the first
general-purpose credit score.

43
00:02:37,485 --> 00:02:38,906
The FICO score.

44
00:02:39,315 --> 00:02:45,196
From 300, to 850. One number,
instead of a stranger's opinion.

45
00:02:45,256 --> 00:02:50,436
Then in 1995, Fannie Mae and Freddie
Mac started using it for mortgages.

46
00:02:50,456 --> 00:02:54,747
And almost overnight, that number
started deciding who gets a home.

47
00:02:55,308 --> 00:03:00,447
Today, FICO says 90% of top
lenders in America use its scores.

48
00:03:04,117 --> 00:03:06,817
So what actually goes into that number?

49
00:03:06,837 --> 00:03:09,017
FICO publishes the recipe.

50
00:03:09,196 --> 00:03:13,597
About 35% is payment
history. Did you pay on time?

51
00:03:13,776 --> 00:03:18,436
About 30% is how much you owe, mostly
how much of your available credit

52
00:03:18,456 --> 00:03:19,516
you're using.

53
00:03:19,536 --> 00:03:23,036
Have a $10,000 limit and a $3,000 balance?

54
00:03:23,056 --> 00:03:27,396
That's thirty percent. Lower usually
scores better, even if you pay it off

55
00:03:27,416 --> 00:03:29,007
in full every month.

56
00:03:29,247 --> 00:03:32,027
15% is how long you've had credit.

57
00:03:32,047 --> 00:03:36,795
10% is new credit. And 10%
is your mix of accounts.

58
00:03:36,815 --> 00:03:39,755
Now notice what's missing. Your income.

59
00:03:39,775 --> 00:03:43,013
Your savings. How much
cash you have in the bank.

60
00:03:43,192 --> 00:03:45,972
The score doesn't measure
whether you're good with money.

61
00:03:45,992 --> 00:03:48,131
It measures how you handle debt.

62
00:03:48,151 --> 00:03:52,691
Which creates a strange trap. If
you've never borrowed, the system can't

63
00:03:52,711 --> 00:03:53,602
see you.

64
00:03:53,741 --> 00:03:58,654
The government counted 26 million
Americans with no credit history at all,

65
00:03:58,674 --> 00:04:03,342
and another 19 million whose files
were too thin or too old to score.

66
00:04:03,461 --> 00:04:08,041
Pay cash for everything your whole
life, and on paper, you can look riskier

67
00:04:08,061 --> 00:04:10,541
than someone juggling three credit cards.

68
00:04:10,730 --> 00:04:15,711
And that's not a bug. To understand
why, you have to follow the money.

69
00:04:19,835 --> 00:04:22,201
So who's making money from all this?

70
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:27,901
Three companies hold credit files on
most adults in America: Equifax, Experian

71
00:04:27,921 --> 00:04:29,333
and TransUnion.

72
00:04:29,472 --> 00:04:33,833
Together, they bring in
about $18 billion a year.

73
00:04:34,013 --> 00:04:38,400
Equifax alone brought in
about $6 billion last year.

74
00:04:38,420 --> 00:04:40,640
And here's the part most people miss.

75
00:04:40,660 --> 00:04:45,280
You're not their customer. The banks,
landlords and insurers who buy your

76
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:46,492
file are.

77
00:04:46,621 --> 00:04:49,481
Ever get a pre-approved
credit card offer in the mail?

78
00:04:49,501 --> 00:04:53,721
The law lets the bureaus sell lists
of names to lenders, sorted by how you

79
00:04:53,741 --> 00:04:54,981
look on paper.

80
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:57,260
And you can't make them delete your file.

81
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:59,847
You can freeze it. You can dispute it.

82
00:04:59,867 --> 00:05:01,260
But it's theirs.

83
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:05,980
And it goes way beyond credit. Equifax
runs a database called The Work Number,

84
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,220
built from employer payroll records.

85
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:13,991
Hundreds of millions of them. Job titles,
start dates, and often, your salary.

86
00:05:14,021 --> 00:05:17,601
Then there's FICO, which gets
paid when lenders pull your score.

87
00:05:17,621 --> 00:05:22,611
In 2025, it raised the price
of a mortgage score by 41%.

88
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,410
And those apps that show
you your score for free?

89
00:05:25,430 --> 00:05:29,543
Credit Karma sold to
Intuit for $7.1 billion.

90
00:05:29,563 --> 00:05:31,490
It makes its money recommending you…

91
00:05:31,510 --> 00:05:33,710
more credit cards, and loans.

92
00:05:33,889 --> 00:05:38,349
And the score in that app? It's usually
a VantageScore, not the FICO versions

93
00:05:38,369 --> 00:05:41,469
most lenders pull. There
are dozens of versions.

94
00:05:41,489 --> 00:05:44,710
So the number you check isn't
always the number they see.

95
00:05:49,170 --> 00:05:51,970
Now here's the part that should worry you.

96
00:05:52,089 --> 00:05:55,989
When the Federal Trade Commission
checked, about one in five people had

97
00:05:56,009 --> 00:05:59,169
an error on at least one
of their credit reports.

98
00:05:59,308 --> 00:06:02,928
And for about one in twenty, the
errors were bad enough that they could

99
00:06:02,948 --> 00:06:04,533
pay more for a loan.

100
00:06:04,672 --> 00:06:08,692
And if you dispute it? Your letter
often gets boiled down to a short code

101
00:06:08,712 --> 00:06:10,932
and sent to whoever reported the debt.

102
00:06:10,952 --> 00:06:14,536
Many times, they simply confirm
it, and the error stays.

103
00:06:14,556 --> 00:06:18,736
Even medical bills counted. Paid-off
hospital debt could sit on your report

104
00:06:18,756 --> 00:06:24,336
for years. Only in 2022 did the
bureaus start removing it, and by 2023,

105
00:06:24,356 --> 00:06:27,878
nearly 70% of medical
collection accounts were gone.

106
00:06:27,898 --> 00:06:32,049
And remember Equifax?
In 2017, it was hacked.

107
00:06:32,069 --> 00:06:37,729
Names, birth dates and Social Security
numbers of 147 million people.

108
00:06:37,749 --> 00:06:38,915
Exposed.

109
00:06:39,064 --> 00:06:44,054
Equifax agreed to pay up
to $700M in settlements.

110
00:06:44,264 --> 00:06:47,044
You never chose to give Equifax that data.

111
00:06:47,064 --> 00:06:49,340
And you couldn't take it back.

112
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:51,819
But the biggest cost isn't a hack.

113
00:06:51,839 --> 00:06:54,926
It's quieter, and you pay it every month.

114
00:06:58,606 --> 00:07:01,866
So what is that number
actually worth to you?

115
00:07:02,035 --> 00:07:07,055
Take a $300,000 mortgage. A lower
score can mean a rate about a point and

116
00:07:07,075 --> 00:07:08,462
a half higher.

117
00:07:08,631 --> 00:07:15,711
That's roughly $300 more every month,
and over $100,000 across thirty years.

118
00:07:16,011 --> 00:07:20,690
Same house. Same person. A
different three-digit number.

119
00:07:20,710 --> 00:07:25,410
And it doesn't stop at loans. In most
states, car insurers can use a credit-based

120
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:29,330
score to set your price. And employers
can check your credit report, with

121
00:07:29,350 --> 00:07:31,766
your permission, before they hire you.

122
00:07:31,786 --> 00:07:35,987
The good news? You have more
power here than they advertise.

123
00:07:36,176 --> 00:07:41,676
You can check all three of your reports for
free, every week, at AnnualCreditReport.com.

124
00:07:41,696 --> 00:07:43,676
You can freeze your credit for free.

125
00:07:43,696 --> 00:07:47,957
And you can opt out of those
mailers at OptOutPrescreen.com.

126
00:07:48,144 --> 00:07:52,847
And the basics still work: pay on
time, keep your balances low, and think

127
00:07:52,867 --> 00:07:55,427
twice before closing your oldest card.

128
00:07:56,056 --> 00:08:00,733
Because here's the truth. The credit
score was never really about you.

129
00:08:00,921 --> 00:08:05,381
It was built to answer one question for
the people lending money: how profitable

130
00:08:05,401 --> 00:08:06,223
are you?

131
00:08:06,253 --> 00:08:10,473
So, here's my question. Should companies
you never signed up with get to

132
00:08:10,493 --> 00:08:14,793
decide what your life costs? Or is
this the fairest system we've got?

133
00:08:14,813 --> 00:08:16,214
Tell me in the comments.

134
00:08:16,919 --> 00:08:19,974
Subscribe for more of the
money hiding in plain sight.

135
00:08:19,994 --> 00:08:22,304
And tonight… check your number.
